      "reason": "No free Marketing Cloud Engagement developer sandbox exists (an Installed Package + a real, paid tenant is required to obtain client_id/client_secret at all — confirmed structurally by authenticate-soap-api.json''s own onboarding instructions: \"Obtain a client ID and secret by creating a package in Marketing Cloud with an API Integration component\", which presupposes an existing paid Marketing Cloud account; no self-serve trial-signup page was located in the pinned corpus).",
      "consequence": "The credential-free ceiling is the honest ceiling for this connector build. Lifting it requires a real tenant Installed Package plus an sfmc-readonly broker plan. RECORDED so this is never rediscovered."
    },
    {
      "id": "tenant-subdomained-hosts",
      "scope": "vendor-wide",
      "reason": "No tenant host resolves without a credential — a bogus subdomain does not even resolve (NXDOMAIN) against *.auth/*.rest/*.soap.marketingcloudapis.com.",
      "consequence": "Credential-free probing is limited to the legacy tenant-agnostic gateway at www.exacttargetapis.com, and ONLY at SERVICE-PREFIX granularity (confirmed live during Stage-2 independent review: real service prefixes [hub/v1, asset/v1, contacts/v1, interaction/v1, automation/v1, data/v1, messaging/v1, platform/v1] returned HTTP 401 Not Authenticated; a bogus prefix/version returned HTTP 596 Service Not Found per error-handling.json''s own table; a bogus LEAF under a real prefix [/hub/v1/notarealthing] ALSO returned 401 — i.e. the gateway confirms a service exists but cannot confirm any specific leaf path exists credential-free). See INDEPENDENT_REVIEW.md ''Gateway probe discrimination'' row."
    },
    {
      "id": "no-authoritative-enumeration",
      "scope": "vendor-wide except DE tier",
      "reason": "No describe-ALL-objects endpoint exists; SOAP Describe/REST discovery each cover their own slice (see DiscoveryIsAuthoritativeNote).",
      "consequence": "Never deactivate an IO or a discovered record on absence from any single discovery call, except a Data Extension itself, which the SOAP Retrieve DataExtension call CAN authoritatively enumerate for one business unit."
    },
    {
      "id": "split-protocol-no-parity",
      "scope": "vendor-wide",
      "reason": "The vendor''s own guidance states existing SOAP integrations and new REST surfaces are not full substitutes for one another (\"existing SOAP API integrations only function with the Classic tools\").",
      "consequence": "Some families are SOAP-only (all 10 tracking-event types, Classic content, generic DE row CRUD''s PRIMARY path) and a REST-only connector would silently omit them. Both transports must be implemented."
    },
    {
      "id": "no-numeric-rate-limit-documented",
      "scope": "vendor-wide",
      "reason": "rate-limiting.json / rate-limiting-errors.json / rate-limiting-best-practices.json all describe throttling as reactive and account-specific (\"Marketing Cloud reserves the right to throttle... depends on the rate necessary to stabilize operations\") with NO published numeric requests-per-second/minute ceiling anywhere in the pinned corpus.",
      "consequence": "BatchMaxRequestCount/BatchRequestWaitTime are left NULL on this Integration row rather than fabricated. Inventing a number would be a fabricated capability claim."
    },
    {
      "id": "throttle-appears-as-500-soap-429-rest",
      "scope": "transport-specific",
      "reason": "SOAP throttling surfaces as HTTP 500 with a SOAP fault payload (rate-limiting.json); REST throttling surfaces as HTTP 429 with a structured JSON body and often Retry-After (rate-limiting-errors.json). These are two DIFFERENT vendor pages with two DIFFERENT documented statuses — see ErrorModel.note for the explicit correction of a plan-time assumption that this was a single vendor-wide 500 rule.",
      "consequence": "ClassifyError must apply adaptive backoff on SOAP 500 (inspecting the fault detail before deciding fatal-vs-retryable) AND on REST 429/Retry-After, as two distinct branches, not one."
    },
    {
      "id": "tenant-defined-data-extensions",
      "scope": "vendor-wide",
      "reason": "Data Extension schemas (and Contact attribute sets) are entirely customer-defined at runtime; no public doc enumerates any tenant''s actual DE columns.",
      "consequence": "Field/object absence for a specific tenant''s Data Extension proves nothing about the vendor-wide mechanism; DE column discovery is a connector runtime concern, not a metadata-authoring concern."
    },
    {
      "id": "short-lived-tokens",
      "scope": "vendor-wide",
      "reason": "Vendor best-practice guidance ties token reuse to a ~20-minute cadence (\"Don''t request more than one access token every 20 minutes\", rate-limiting-best-practices.json) and directs callers to the expires_in response parameter for the authoritative value.",
      "consequence": "Proactive refresh (using expires_in, refreshing ahead of expiry) is mandatory for any sync longer than ~20 minutes; never rely on a 401 to discover expiry."
    },
    {
      "id": "rest-de-rowset-filtering-asymmetry",
      "scope": "REST Data Extension rowset endpoints",
      "reason": "UNPROVEN. Planning-time external research surfaced a claim that non-sendable Data Extensions cap REST rowset retrieval at 200 rows while sendable ones do not; a targeted grep of the pinned Atlas corpus''s postDataExtensionRowsetByKey/getResultsDataExtensionAsync pages for an explicit sendable-vs-non-sendable row-cap statement did NOT turn up a load-bearing sentence in this pass (SOURCE_STUDY.md Residual Doubt #6). Also relevant: the REST rowset endpoint path itself was not located as a titled page at all in this pinned snapshot (see DynamicObjectModel.rowRead.alternateEvidence).",
      "consequence": "Do NOT branch connector logic on sendable-vs-non-sendable DE filtering behavior until independently confirmed against a live account. Recorded as UNPROVEN, not as a fact."
    },
    {
      "id": "no-official-openapi",
      "scope": "REST transport",
      "reason": "No official vendor-published OpenAPI/Swagger spec exists for the REST surface; sfmc-engagement-openapi.derived.json is SCRIPT-DERIVED from the Atlas prose docs + CRUD matrix, not vendor-authored.",
      "consequence": "T7 (spec-conformance verification) is LINT here — validated against the derived artifact''s own internal consistency and against the live Atlas prose — not external verification against a vendor contract."
    }
  ],
  "APIVersioningStrategy": "url-path",
  "APIVersioningStrategyNote": "URL-path segment, but NOT globally uniform: all REST resource-family paths observed carry /v1/ immediately after the service prefix (hub/v1, asset/v1, contacts/v1, interaction/v1, automation/v1, data/v1, messaging/v1, platform/v1, sms/v1, push/v1 — 10 distinct service prefixes observed across sources/rest-parsed.json). The Auth service specifically uses /v2/ (v2/token, v2/userinfo, v2/discovery, routes.json) — a different version number under a different service. Do not assume a single vendor-wide version constant.",
  "CustomObjectMarkerPattern": null,
  "CustomObjectMarkerPatternGap": "No naming-convention marker for custom objects was located. Unlike e.g. HubSpot''s p_<accountID>_<name>, SFMC''s customer-custom-object mechanism (Data Extensions) has no distinguishing name prefix — a Data Extension IS the custom-object primitive itself, not a marked subset of a larger object catalog. Left NULL; see DynamicObjectModel for the actual mechanism.",
  "CustomFieldMarkerPattern": null,
  "CustomFieldMarkerPatternGap": "No naming-convention marker for custom fields was located in the pinned corpus. Left NULL.",
  "WebhooksAvailable": true,
  "WebhooksSignatureAlgorithm": "HMAC-SHA256",
  "WebhooksSignatureHeader": "x-sfmc-ens-signature",
  "WebhooksEvidence": "ens-notification-signing.json — explicit statement: \"Marketing Cloud Event Notification Service signs every notification delivered to your callback with an HMAC-SHA256 signature... compare the HMAC-SHA256 signature to the value in x-sfmc-ens-signature.\" ENS callback/subscription CONFIGURATION is in-scope as CRUD records (createCallback/createSubscription/etc.); the inbound webhook DELIVERY transport itself is out of scope (see OutOfScopeObjectFamilies).",
  "BulkOperationsAvailable": true,
  "BulkOperationsBasePath": "/data/v1/async",
  "BulkOperationsEvidence": "sources/rest-parsed.json — insertDataExtensionIDAsync (POST /data/v1/async/dataextensions/{id}/rows) and updateDataExtensionIDAsync (PUT, same path) submit a job; getStatusDataExtensionAsync (GET /data/v1/async/{requestId}/status) and getResultsDataExtensionAsync (GET /data/v1/async/{requestId}/results) poll it — a genuine async submit-then-poll pattern, distinct from the SOAP-side Perform/Schedule/Configure action verbs (see ActionCatalogSummary).",
  "TokenRefreshStrategy": "proactive-before-expiry",
  "TokenRefreshStrategyNote": "See Auth.refreshPolicy/Auth.tokenLifetimeSeconds for the full evidence — restated here at the top level because TokenRefreshStrategy is named as its own Configuration slot in the extraction directive.",
  "WriteCapability": {
    "soap": "Create/Update/Delete verbs exist per the CRUD matrix (sources/soap-crud-matrix.json, 85 unique object names with Create/Retrieve/Update/Delete/Perform/Schedule/Configure columns) — write support is genuinely PER-OBJECT, not uniform. Tracking events (SentEvent, OpenEvent, etc.) are Retrieve-only by their nature (they are system-generated log records); DataExtensionObject/DataExtension/Contact-adjacent objects (Subscriber, List) support Create/Update/Delete per the matrix.",
    "rest": "Create/Update(PATCH or PUT)/Delete verbs observed per-resource in sources/rest-parsed.json (Methods: GET 75 / POST 76 / PATCH 5 / PUT 13 / DELETE 20) — again per-object, not uniform. Some REST families are read-only or action-only (see OutOfScopeObjectFamilies action-not-record-crud entry and ActionCatalogSummary).",
    "note": "Per-IO WriteCapability (hasCreate/hasUpdate/hasDelete) is captured per-object in sources/capabilities.json by ioiof-extractor''s own evidence; this vendor-level note establishes that write capability gates on the OBJECT, never assume vendor-wide uniform CRUD."
  },
  "ConcurrencyControl": "none",
  "ConcurrencyControlEvidence": "A corpus-wide scan of all 865 pinned Atlas pages (excluding manifest.json) for the literal strings ''ETag'' and ''If-Match'' returned 0 hits on both (see CODE_EVIDENCE — extract-sfmc-facts.py concurrencyControlScan). No optimistic-concurrency header or version-token mechanism is documented anywhere in the pinned corpus for either transport. Journeys (Interactions) carry their own domain-specific versionNumber field (see deleteInteractionById.json), but that is a JOURNEY-VERSIONING feature (multiple named versions of one journey), not a generic optimistic-concurrency control usable for conflict detection on arbitrary writes.",
  "ConcurrencyControlConsequence": "No real MostRecent conflict resolution is possible from vendor-native concurrency tokens; conflict handling, if bidirectional is ever enabled, must be snapshot-3-way only (compare last-synced snapshot vs. current source vs. current destination), never an ETag/If-Match-based optimistic check.",
  "DeleteSemantics": {
    "default": "varies-by-object — NOT vendor-uniform, see per-family notes below",
    "contacts": {
      "shape": "async two-phase soft-then-hard",
      "evidence": "DeleteByContactIDs.json (POST /contacts/v1/contacts/actions/delete?type=ids) — EXPLICIT vendor statement: the call is asynchronous and QUEUED (''each process can take several hours to complete''); phase 1 is a SUPPRESSION (''A successful call suppresses any sends or other activities to the specified contacts for a specified number of days... Users may still see the contact record''); phase 2 after the configurable suppression window is an IRREVERSIBLE hard delete (''Marketing Cloud deletes the contact from all lists and sendable data extensions. You cannot restore any contact information associated with this delete process''). The suppression window is tenant-configurable via a separate ''Configure Settings for Deleting Contacts'' resource.",
      "softDeleteFlagField": "not a boolean flag — the tombstone state is implicit in the suppression window, not an inspectable field on the Contact record itself as far as this pinned corpus shows"
    },
    "dataExtensionRows": {
      "shape": "unproven-assume-hard",
      "evidence": "No explicit soft-delete/tombstone statement was located for SOAP DataExtensionObject Delete or the REST rowset delete path in this pass. Treat as immediate hard delete until independently confirmed; do NOT assume orphan-deletion is safe without a live-account check."
    },
    "genericSoapObjects": {
      "shape": "unproven-per-object",
      "evidence": "The CRUD matrix documents WHICH objects support a Delete verb (85 rows) but not the tombstone-vs-hard-delete SEMANTICS of that verb per object; this is a per-IO extraction task, not resolvable vendor-wide from the pinned corpus."
    },
    "consequence": "Orphan-deletion propagation must default OFF except where an object''s DeleteSemantics is independently proven hard/soft with evidence. Contacts specifically require respecting the tenant''s configured suppression window before treating a delete as final."
  },
  "DefaultSyncDirection": "Pull",
  "DefaultSyncDirectionNote": "Read-only Pull is the recommended default for EVERY object in this catalog per this build''s read-first, bidirectional-opt-in discipline. This is independent of any per-object WriteCapability finding above — write CAPABILITY existing at the vendor does not make write DEFAULT-safe against a client''s real Marketing Cloud tenant.",
  "DefaultConflictResolution": "Manual",
  "DefaultConflictResolutionNote": "IF bidirectional sync is later enabled for a specific object (opt-in, never default), the recommended conflict-resolution policy is Manual, NOT MostRecent — because ConcurrencyControl is ''none'' (no ETag/If-Match/version token found anywhere in the pinned corpus, see ConcurrencyControlEvidence), so there is no vendor-native mechanism to reliably determine which side is actually ''most recent'' at write time. DestWins/SourceWins are also not recommended as defaults because SFMC Contact deletes are irreversible after the suppression window (see DeleteSemantics.contacts) and SOAP writes on tracking-derived objects would corrupt system-of-record log data — a human must arbitrate. Preconditions before ANY bidirectional default could be safely reconsidered for a specific object: (1) that object''s WriteCapability is independently proven (not just CRUD-matrix-declared) against a live account, (2) that object''s DeleteSemantics is independently proven (hard vs. soft, and for Contacts specifically the exact suppression-window behavior) against a live account, (3) a sandbox validation pass exists (this vendor has NO free sandbox — see DeclaredCapabilityLimits no-free-sandbox — so this precondition is currently UNMET for every object in this catalog and bidirectional must stay opt-in-with-explicit-allowWrite for the foreseeable future).",
